{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "02-functions.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Functions",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Define functions of one or more inputs, apply them, read their types, and choose between results with `if`.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A definition can take inputs. Each is written in parentheses with its type, and the result's type comes after the colon:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "def square (n : Nat) : Nat := n * n\n\n#eval square 7\n#eval square (3 + 4)",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A function is applied by writing the input after it, with a space: `square 7`, not `square(7)`. Parentheses only group: `square (3 + 4)` squares 7, while `square 3 + 4` would square 3 and then add 4.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A function of two inputs takes them one after the other:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "def area (w h : Nat) : Nat := w * h\n\n#eval area 3 4\n#check (area)\n#check (square)",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "`square` has the type `Nat → Nat`: it takes a `Nat` to a `Nat`. `area` has `Nat → Nat → Nat`: give it a `Nat`, then another, and you get a `Nat`.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Functions without names",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "`fun x => …` is a function with no name, handy where a function is needed only once, such as for `List.map`, which applies a function to every element of a list:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "#eval (fun x => x + 1) 5\n#eval [1, 2, 3].map square\n#eval [1, 2, 3].map (fun x => 10 * x)",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Choosing with if",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "`if … then … else …` chooses between two results. Both must have the same type, and the `else` is not optional: a function must give a result for every input.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "def absDiff (a b : Nat) : Nat := if a < b then b - a else a - b\n\n#eval absDiff 3 10\n#eval absDiff 10 3",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Exercises",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "As before: write the definition, and the check below the statement computes the cases it lists.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem cube_ok : cube 2 = 8 ∧ cube 3 = 27 ∧ cube 10 = 1000 := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "Define `cube`, which takes `n` to $n^3$.",
      "leanSolution": "  decide",
      "leanStart": "  decide",
      "hints": [
        "Look at `square` and multiply once more."
      ],
      "leanDef": "def cube (n : Nat) : Nat := sorry",
      "leanDefSolution": "def cube (n : Nat) : Nat := n * n * n",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem max3_ok : max3 1 5 3 = 5 ∧ max3 9 2 4 = 9 ∧ max3 2 2 7 = 7 ∧ max3 4 4 4 = 4 := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "Define `max3`, the largest of three numbers, with `if`. (Lean's `max` is ruled out.)",
      "leanSolution": "  decide",
      "leanStart": "  decide",
      "hints": [
        "One `if` can be inside another: `if a < b then (if b < c then c else b) else …`.",
        "When `a` is not less than `b`, compare `a` with `c`."
      ],
      "leanDef": "def max3 (a b c : Nat) : Nat := sorry",
      "leanDefSolution": "def max3 (a b c : Nat) : Nat := if a < b then (if b < c then c else b) else (if a < c then c else a)",
      "leanForbid": [
        "max",
        "Max.max"
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem isEven_ok : isEven 4 = true ∧ isEven 7 = false ∧ isEven 0 = true := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "Define `isEven`, which says whether a number is even, as a `Bool`.",
      "leanSolution": "  decide",
      "leanStart": "  decide",
      "hints": [
        "`n % 2` is the remainder on dividing by 2.",
        "`==` compares and gives a `Bool`."
      ],
      "leanDef": "def isEven (n : Nat) : Bool := sorry",
      "leanDefSolution": "def isEven (n : Nat) : Bool := n % 2 == 0",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> `def f (x : A) : B := …` is a function from `A` to `B`, applied as `f x`; its type is `A → B`, and a function of two inputs has `A → B → C`. `fun x => …` is one without a name. `if … then … else …` chooses, and must give a result either way.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Every function so far does a fixed amount of work. The next lesson's functions call themselves, which is how a function handles a number or a list of any size.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
